{"team":"ATL","col":"#FF525B","identity":"run-heavy","funnel":"run-funnel","plays_pg":62.0,"adot":7.9,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>Bleed the Clock</h2><p>Run identity is the through-line here: ATL call pass on <strong>-7%</strong> of snaps relative to what the situation expects (league -2%), and when they get ahead they lean even harder on the ground \u2014 just 33% pass on early downs with a lead. This is a ball-control operation built to shorten games.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>Their run game attacks the perimeter \u2014 <strong>32% of carries hit the edge</strong> (league ~25%), an outside-zone lean that rewards speed backs and can boom for chunk gains.</p><p>They live in the shotgun (81% of snaps vs ~68%) \u2014 a spread, pass-oriented structure.</p><p><strong>Kyle Pitts</strong> is a genuine centerpiece, not an afterthought \u2014 <strong>18%</strong> of targets (league ~15%).</p><p>By the numbers it's an efficient unit \u2014 a 46% success rate (above the 44% average).</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Inside the 20 they lean run-heavy, passing <strong>46%</strong> of the time, funnelling touchdown equity to <strong>Bijan Robinson</strong>. And with the TE commanding 18% of targets, <strong>Kyle Pitts</strong> is a live red-zone option too.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 The Run Comes Free</h2><p>They surrender <strong>124 rushing</strong> and <strong>229 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). The run moves against them \u2014 <strong>target opposing RB1s</strong>.</p><p>They lock down opposing WR1s \u2014 just 52 rec yds/game (vs ~58); fade receiving props there.</p><p>They leak <strong>WR1 receiving TDs</strong> (0.47/game vs ~0.38) \u2014 an anytime-TD spot to attack.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: back <strong>Bijan Robinson</strong>'s rushing volume in favored/positive scripts; <strong>opposing RB1s</strong> smash on the ground and at the goal line.</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; the profile rebuilds through